 # Hierarchical MPI Image Reduction
 
-Also called: MPI Reduce for Volume Rendering, Tree-based Image Compositing
+Also called: Binary Tree Image Composition, MPI Reduce for Volume Rendering, Tree-based Image Compositing
 
 ## Overview
-Hierarchical MPI Image Reduction is a parallel compositing strategy that combines per-process rendered sub-images into a single final image using a binary tree reduction over MPI ranks [S1, unofficial]. It addresses the bottleneck of sending all partial images to a single root process for final assembly, which causes network congestion and memory pressure on the root [S1, unofficial]. Unlike flat reductions, this method distributes the compositing load across the process hierarchy, reducing the communication depth and balancing the workload [S1, unofficial]. It builds on [Parallel Image Compositing with Overlapping](3554-parallel-image-compositing-with-overlapping.md) by handling the final aggregation of ray segments or sub-images after local computation.
+Hierarchical MPI Image Reduction is a parallel compositing strategy that combines per-process rendered sub-images into a single final image using a binary tree reduction over MPI ranks [S1, unofficial]. It addresses the bottleneck of sending all partial images to a single root process for final assembly, which causes network congestion and memory pressure on the root [S1, unofficial]. Unlike flat reductions, this method distributes the compositing load across the process hierarchy, reducing the communication depth and balancing the workload [S1, unofficial].
 
